<h1>Monday Playbook</h1>
<div class="dates">Your picks from the forensic report · Week of Mon 24 Aug -&gt; Fri 28 Aug 2026</div></div>
<p class="pk-intro">Every concrete play from the report, one card each. Tap <b>Shadow</b>, <b>Live</b>, or <b>Skip</b> for each. Your picks save automatically — when you're done, hit <b>Copy my picks</b> or <b>Send to Telegram</b> and they come back to me for the shadow-vs-live conversation before Monday. <b>Refuted &amp; exploratory plays can't go Live</b> until they survive a week in shadow — that guardrail is built in.</p>
<div class="pk-group">✅ Act on these — survived proof &amp; real bugs</div>
<div class="pk-card" data-id="timer-the-two-dead-nightlies-0828" data-default="live"><div class="pk-top"><span class="pk-ref">SATURDAY #1 · Part 2.6 §honesty-flag-1 and recommendation #1 (confidence: CERTAIN). · Put router_nightly.py and selector_nightly.py on a timer — both have been silently dead for a fortnight</span><span class="pill v-verified">✓ VERIFIED</span></div><div class="pk-play">Write a systemd timer for each (or one timer that runs both), 22:30Z daily, after the Paris-day roll. They are pure rollups: no orders, no switches, no risk.</div><div class="pk-meta">Neither script has a systemd unit or a crontab entry anywhere on the box. The only things that reference them are the Friday build scripts, so they have effectively been running once a week, by accident, inside the report. When Part 2.6 went looking this cycle, the newest data/router_nightly/*.json was dated 2026-08-14 and the newest selector file 2026-08-18. · This is the report&#x27;s own instrumentation, and the report is the thing that noticed. Every nightly number in Part 2.6 this week is a REBUILD done during the report, not the output of a monitor that was watching — so the desk has had no daily read on router value or exit selection for 10–14 days and nothing said so. · The number: router_nightly last wrote 2026-08-14, selector_nightly 2026-08-18 — 14 and 10 days dead. Zero units in /etc/systemd/system, zero crontab entries. Part 2.6 rebuilt all five days by hand to write its section. · Proven wrong by: systemctl list-timers | grep nightly shows both, and a file dated tomorrow appears in data/router_nightly/ without anyone running the Friday build.</div><div class="pk-opts"><button class="pk-opt" data-mode="shadow">Shadow</button><button class="pk-opt" data-mode="live">Live</button><button class="pk-opt" data-mode="skip">Skip</button></div></div>
<div class="pk-card" data-id="alert-on-router-headless-aborts-0828" data-default="live"><div class="pk-top"><span class="pk-ref">SATURDAY #2 · Part 2.6 recommendation #2 (CERTAIN, measured on four separate days); Part 1 §5 counts the same 43 from the tournament&#x27;s side. · ★ The router went blind for 3h35m and every health instrument on the box said it was fine</span><span class="pill v-verified">✓ VERIFIED</span></div><div class="pk-play">Point the health alert at data/router_headless.log ABORT lines rather than at the trial log, and fix the health page&#x27;s text — it currently blames an &quot;expired login&quot; for what is a usage limit resetting on the hour.</div><div class="pk-meta">43 ticks aborted this cycle. An abort is fail-closed — it writes nothing — which is the correct default and is exactly why it is invisible: the trial log gets no line at all, systemctl reports the unit as cleanly deactivated, and the tick count reads normal. 31 of the 43 are the LLM session limit; the other 12 are all Monday, inside the 04:28–05:00Z gateway window — 4 invocation failures plus 8 empty responses, a second failure mode with the same silent signature. · A monitor that cannot see its subject&#x27;s most common failure is not a monitor. The desk&#x27;s #1 lever was absent for three and a half hours and the only evidence is a log nothing watches. · The number: 43 ABORTs ≈ 3h35m blind. 31 session-limit, 12 Monday-gateway. The −$135.43 Friday miss landed inside a blackout. systemctl / trial log / tick count all read clean throughout. · Proven wrong by: Kill the router mid-tick and confirm a page arrives naming the abort, not silence.</div><div class="pk-opts"><button class="pk-opt" data-mode="shadow">Shadow</button><button class="pk-opt" data-mode="live">Live</button><button class="pk-opt" data-mode="skip">Skip</button></div></div>
<div class="pk-card" data-id="open-hour-watch-must-match-its-unit-0828" data-default="live"><div class="pk-top"><span class="pk-ref">SATURDAY #3 · Part 2.6 recommendation #7 (HIGH); Part 1 §4 shows the watcher as one of only three writers of gate_switches.env this week. · Make open_hour_watch.py match its own unit file, or give its prompt header an EXPIRY</span><span class="pill v-verified">✓ VERIFIED</span></div><div class="pk-play">Either remove the gate-switch write path from open_hour_watch.py so it is genuinely alert-only as its unit file promises, or — if the auto-arm is wanted — change the unit file to say so and add a hard expiry to the header notes it injects.</div><div class="pk-meta">open-hour-watch.service states the process &quot;must NEVER write gate_switches.env&quot;. It wrote twice this week, and on Friday one of those writes re-armed a gate the router had deliberately benched 9 minutes earlier. Separately, the note it splices into the router&#x27;s prompt carries no expiry, so it sat in every router prompt for 3h51m after being reversed. · Two independent hazards in one file, and they compound: a second writer with no standdown awareness, plus a no-expiry instruction that outlives the situation it described. Obeying the stale note would have cost −$258.06. · The number: 2 writes this week against a unit file that forbids them; a 9-minute override of a live router bench; a header note live for 3h51m past its reversal, worth −$258.06 if obeyed. · Proven wrong by: grep -n &#x27;gate_switches&#x27; scripts/open_hour_watch.py returns nothing, or the unit file no longer claims it cannot write.</div><div class="pk-opts"><button class="pk-opt" data-mode="shadow">Shadow</button><button class="pk-opt" data-mode="live">Live</button><button class="pk-opt" data-mode="skip">Skip</button></div></div>
<div class="pk-card" data-id="router-step-15m-to-5m-0828" data-default="live"><div class="pk-top"><span class="pk-ref">MONDAY #2 · Part 2.6 recommendation #3 (HIGH) and Part 1 §5 / §9, which price the same arm from the tournament&#x27;s side and agree on the direction. · ★ Attack the 20-minute arm lag — the router&#x27;s dominant error is no longer direction, it is LATENESS</span><span class="pill v-verified">✓ VERIFIED</span></div><div class="pk-play">Change the router step from 15m to 5m (the timing half of the sweep winner) and add one explicit prompt line: arm on the FIRST confirmed break impulse, not the fifth.</div><div class="pk-meta">Decomposed, roughly 15 of the 20 minutes is the router&#x27;s own confirmation appetite and only ~5 is the tick grid — so the step change alone does not fix it, which is why the prompt line ships with it. Actuation once decided is not the problem: on Friday the gates filled 1.4 seconds after the switch flipped. · This is the week&#x27;s clearest measured cost and it points the same way from two independent sections. The arm that made the week&#x27;s money was right — and late. · The number: Friday 14:45:03Z arm: measured lateness $205–$705 against the $181.50 the arm banked. Part 1 prices the same delay on one lot at $33.84 (grind_long&#x27;s own 14:36:26Z suppressed signal was worth +$150.02; the fill 8m37s later was worth +$116.18). · Proven wrong by: router_trial_log.txt shows ticks 5 minutes apart, and the next arm&#x27;s gap between first suppressed signal and switch write is under 5 minutes.</div><div class="pk-opts"><button class="pk-opt" data-mode="shadow">Shadow</button><button class="pk-opt" data-mode="live">Live</button><button class="pk-opt" data-mode="skip">Skip</button></div></div>
<div class="pk-card" data-id="router-must-quote-window-and-family-0828" data-default="live"><div class="pk-top"><span class="pk-ref">MONDAY #3 · Part 2.6 recommendation #6 (CERTAIN). Part 2 §2 finds the same class of fault in the tooling (abs_veto_sides.py hard-codes WEEK0 = 2026-07-27 and reports 4½ weeks as &quot;this week&quot;). · Make the router quote a WINDOW and a FAMILY whenever it cites the shadow board</span><span class="pill v-verified">✓ VERIFIED</span></div><div class="pk-play">One line in the router prompt: any shadow-board figure must name its date window and the arm family it covers, or it may not be used as a reason.</div><div class="pk-meta">On 08-28 a bench rested on &quot;344 fader trades at −$3,021 today&quot;. The real figure for that day and family was 128 trades / −$636.50 — the quoted number was an all-time total wearing the word &quot;today&quot;. · The error is not random: an unwindowed aggregate is almost always larger than the windowed one, so it systematically INFLATES the case for benching. Here it inflated it about five-fold and the bench then cost −$135.43. · The number: Claimed 344 trades / −$3,021; actual 128 / −$636.50 — a ~5× overstatement. The bench it justified cost −$135.43. · Proven wrong by: Next router citation of the board includes a date range and an arm name.</div><div class="pk-opts"><button class="pk-opt" data-mode="shadow">Shadow</button><button class="pk-opt" data-mode="live">Live</button><button class="pk-opt" data-mode="skip">Skip</button></div></div>
<div class="pk-card" data-id="untradeable-stop-meter-tournament-only-0828" data-default="live"><div class="pk-top"><span class="pk-ref">MONDAY #4 · Part 2.6 recommendation #5 (CERTAIN). · Filter the untradeable stop-rate meter to tournament gates only — it has never once discriminated</span><span class="pill v-verified">✓ VERIFIED</span></div><div class="pk-play">Restrict the meter&#x27;s stop-rate input to tournament rows. Today it reads the last 15 trades of BOTH books.</div><div class="pk-meta">The day rider&#x27;s exit vocabulary contains no STOP at all, so every rider row it ingests votes zero by construction. On Friday that vote pulled the score 53→42 and flipped the verdict — on an input that was measuring nothing. · A sub-meter that structurally cannot fire is not a conservative input, it is a constant with a vote. Zero of this week&#x27;s 32 trades exited on STOP. · The number: 0 of 32 trades exited STOP this week. Friday: score 53→42 on the rider-dominated sample, verdict flipped. · Proven wrong by: Re-score Friday 08-28 with the filter on and confirm the meter reads 53, not 42.</div><div class="pk-opts"><button class="pk-opt" data-mode="shadow">Shadow</button><button class="pk-opt" data-mode="live">Live</button><button class="pk-opt" data-mode="skip">Skip</button></div></div>
<div class="pk-card" data-id="hold-the-chop-bench-0828" data-default="skip"><div class="pk-top"><span class="pk-ref">HOLD #1 · Part 1 §7 and §8; Movement 2 agrees independently — the six gates fired into this week&#x27;s runs lose −$741.50 on 32 lots. · Keep the chop bench — but stop quoting the dollar figure</span><span class="pill v-verified">✓ VERIFIED</span></div><div class="pk-play">Leave the policy exactly as it is: bench everything with no confirmed direction. And do not quote the $810 at anybody, including yourself.</div><div class="pk-meta">Every benched gate&#x27;s suppressed intents were collapsed to distinct impulses (187) and walked forward on the 250ms tick tape under each gate&#x27;s own live exit. Of the 127 priced signals, the 66 that fired into CHOP account for essentially the whole saving: −$771.78, or −$11.69 a signal, versus a wash in both trend buckets. · The direction is consistent under a lot of ways to break it and the magnitude is not. Drop any single day and the saving is still +$419 to +$955; strip the 3 biggest saves and it is +$455; reprice at 1.0R through 3.0R and it stays positive at all five widths. But the bootstrap band is −$639 to +$2,141 and P(the bench saved nothing or lost money) = 0.13. · The number: 127 priced signals, bench saved +$810.44 (+$6.38/signal). CHOP bucket 66 signals −$771.78. Aligned-trend bucket 50 signals −$0.81/signal — benching in trend is FREE. Label-shuffle placebo on the aligned-vs-chop gap: P = 0.178. It is a candidate, not a finding. · Proven wrong by: Nothing to verify — this is the current policy.</div><div class="pk-opts"><button class="pk-opt" data-mode="shadow">Shadow</button><button class="pk-opt" data-mode="live">Live</button><button class="pk-opt" data-mode="skip">Skip</button></div></div>
<div class="pk-card" data-id="hold-arm-aggressively-in-aligned-trend-0828" data-default="skip"><div class="pk-top"><span class="pk-ref">HOLD #2 · Part 1 §2, §9 and §11 disposition (KEEP LIVE). · Keep the router&#x27;s aligned-momentum arm — and note WHY it stays, which is not the six winners</span><span class="pill v-verified">✓ VERIFIED</span></div><div class="pk-play">Leave the arm behaviour alone: arm grind_long + abs_veto_long into a confirmed aligned break.</div><div class="pk-meta">One arm at Friday 14:45:03Z, four lots in 58 seconds, four winners, +$181.50. Fifty minutes later the router benched both 46.5 points under the day high and the tape then fell 328 points. One arm and one bench, both right, with money on both sides. · ★ It stays live because the benched-signal replay says arming in aligned trend is FREE (−$0.81 per signal over 50 signals) — not because six lots won. Six-for-six on lots that are three gates agreeing about one push, each booking two lots of a scale-out, is n=1 with a wide grin. · The number: 6 lots, 6 winners, +$330.00 for the week — one market event inside 51 minutes. The supporting number is the aligned bucket: 50 benched signals, −$0.81 each. · Proven wrong by: n/a — no change.</div><div class="pk-opts"><button class="pk-opt" data-mode="shadow">Shadow</button><button class="pk-opt" data-mode="live">Live</button><button class="pk-opt" data-mode="skip">Skip</button></div></div>
<div class="pk-card" data-id="hold-the-stop-contract-fix-0828" data-default="skip"><div class="pk-top"><span class="pk-ref">HOLD #3 · Part 1 §10. · Do NOT revert the stop-contract fix — 31 clean days, but read the caveat</span><span class="pill v-verified">✓ VERIFIED</span></div><div class="pk-play">Leave it in production.</div><div class="pk-meta">Every STOP_UNFILLED the desk has ever booked — all 26, worth −$1,377.50 — happened between 21 and 28 July. The last one was a month ago. · The fair caveat is exposure, not time: the tournament has traded 6 lots in the last nine sessions, so the recent stretch of that clean record is thin and largely untested rather than proven. Do not read &quot;31 clean days&quot; as 31 days of exposure. · The number: 21–28 July: 26 STOP_UNFILLED exits, −$1,377.50. 29 July – 28 August: 0. This week: 0. · Proven wrong by: n/a.</div><div class="pk-opts"><button class="pk-opt" data-mode="shadow">Shadow</button><button class="pk-opt" data-mode="live">Live</button><button class="pk-opt" data-mode="skip">Skip</button></div></div>
<div class="pk-card" data-id="hold-adaptive-exit-off-0828" data-default="skip"><div class="pk-top"><span class="pk-ref">HOLD #4 · Part 2 §8–§9 (the exit ladder, a NULL with named tests); Part 2.6 recommendation #9. · Leave adaptive_exit OFF — and leave data/exit_overrides.json alone this weekend</span><span class="pill v-verified">✓ VERIFIED</span></div><div class="pk-play">Change nothing about the exit selector or the per-gate exit cells.</div><div class="pk-meta">All 12 sub-slots already return adaptive_exit=False. On the only day this week with real tournament trades, a fixed always-SCALP exit beat the counterfactual selector by $1,400 (+$799 vs −$600), and 18 of the week&#x27;s 22 &quot;optimal picks&quot; were all-modes-identical and carried no information. · Independently, Part 2&#x27;s exit lab widened the window as far as this desk can go — 831,379 five-second bars, three entry pools including the retired V5 desk&#x27;s real fills, 6,736 scored entries, a 63-cell grid plus six chandeliers — and graded 20 rung-cells. All 20 failed. Not one survived strip-the-best-3, leave-one-day-out, the half-split and the pool-split together. · The number: 20 of 20 rung-cells fail. Selector vs fixed on the only live day: −$600 vs +$799. Written to data/exit_overrides_proposed_v2.json with DEPLOY=false and a cause of death per cell. · Proven wrong by: n/a.</div><div class="pk-opts"><button class="pk-opt" data-mode="shadow">Shadow</button><button class="pk-opt" data-mode="live">Live</button><button class="pk-opt" data-mode="skip">Skip</button></div></div>
